Preconditions:
[PRE:SIGNIN_AS:LOYALTY_VIP_USER]
[PRE:RESET_CART]
@td(MSN_PERSKU_ALL.name) visible. Came from account dashboard (so Back has a real prior page to return to, not the browser's own history boundary).

Assertions:
[NAV] Back lands on {{FRONT_URL}}/account (the page visited before missions), not a broken or blank intermediate state {SPEC}
[STATE] the cart still holds the line written by the modal — the committed mutation is not rolled back by a client-side history navigation {BL}
[NAV] navigating forward again from {{FRONT_URL}}/account back to /account/missions renders the page fresh, with no resurrected stale modal open {SPEC}

[DOM] a graceful re-authentication prompt or a redirect to sign-in is visible, and the raw unhandled 401, a blank white page, and a permanently spinning modal are all absent {BL}
[DOM] no raw GraphQL error object, stack frame or HTTP status code is visible to the customer {BL}
[NAV] after re-authenticating, the customer lands back on /account/missions rather than a generic dashboard, matching MSNF-003's established returnUrl contract for this route {SPEC}

[DOM] the missions page's own heading and its mission card grid are both visible despite the corrupted cart cache, and the points-balance banner (a separate, cart-independent data source) is also visible — a cart-storage fault must not blank an unrelated page region {BL}
[DOM] the modal either opens cleanly (self-healing by refetching cart state from the server rather than trusting the corrupted cache) or fails with a scoped, customer-readable error — a full white screen or an unhandled exception is absent in either case {BL}
[STATE] if the add-to-cart write is attempted, it either succeeds and the cart-cache is repaired to a valid state, or it fails visibly — a write that silently succeeds server-side while the client cache stays corrupted, so the header count is now wrong, is the specific failure this case exists to catch {HYPOTHESIS}
[REL] if the add-to-cart write is attempted and succeeds, the header cart indicator value = the cart page's own total unit count afterward — a self-consistency check that the corrupted cache did not leave the client showing a stale count while the server holds a different one {BL}

[NAV] the anonymous request for /account/missions ends on the route /sign-in, carrying a returnUrl that resolves back to /account/missions, never on a not-found page {BL}
[NAV] the post-login route is {{FRONT_URL}}/account/missions rather than the account dashboard, so the link the customer followed is the page they land on, with no second manual navigation {BL}
[REL] the number of mission cards rendered == the number of mission objects the loyaltyMissionProgress response carries under data {DOC}
[DOM] a card titled @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.name) is present in the grid reached by the redirect {OBSERVED}
[MATH] the reward figure on that card = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.reward) {OBSERVED}
[REL] the progress figure on the card == the progress the same loyaltyMissionProgress response returns for that mission, so the card reports the state the API returned rather than a default {DOC}
[MATH] the goal currency code rendered in the order-goal modal = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.goal_currency), the mission own stored goal currency, not whichever currency the session happens to carry {BL}
[DOM] an entry labelled 'Missions & challenges' is present in the account navigation on the page reached by the redirect {SPEC}

[REL] the number of mission cards rendered == the number of mission objects the loyaltyMissionProgress response carries under data, on every load observed {DOC}
[DOM] while the response carries at least one mission object under data, the whole-page mission-loading error surface is absent and the card grid is visible in its place {SPEC}
[REL] DISCRIMINATING ARM — on a response whose errors[] is non-empty while data still carries mission objects, the rendered card count == that same data count, so a failed nested product sub-field costs the customer that field and never the whole list {DOC}
[DOM] a mission whose nested product sub-field failed still renders its own card, which stays visible in the grid, with the failed sub-field degraded in place rather than the card omitted {SPEC}
[REL] DECIDABILITY GATE — if no response observed in this run carries a non-empty errors[] alongside populated data, the discriminating arm is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET and never PASS: a clean payload takes the same path under either errorPolicy {DOC}

[REL] the set of entries in the account navigation read immediately after the in-session sign-in == the set read after the full page reload of the same URL, so no entry exists only once the application has been bootstrapped a second time {SPEC}
[DOM] an entry labelled 'Missions & challenges' is present in the account navigation before any reload {SPEC}
[REL] the points-history entry appears in the pre-reload navigation as well as in the post-reload one, so the two lists agree on it too {SPEC}
[NAV] activating the missions entry found before the reload lands on {{FRONT_URL}}/account/missions {SPEC}
[REL] POSITIVE CONTROL — the post-reload navigation carries the missions entry; a control that carries no missions entry either means the feature is gated off on this store rather than bootstrapped late, so the case is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET rather than FAIL {DOC}

[DOM] the progress line visible on the card for @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.name) states the spend target as well as the amount accrued so far, so the reader can tell how far from the goal they are without opening the modal {OBSERVED}
[MATH] the target stated on the card = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.goal_target) {OBSERVED}
[REL] POSITIVE CONTROL — the same target value is rendered in the order-goal modal opened from that card, so the value is already carried by the payload the card holds and any omission on the card is a display decision rather than missing data {OBSERVED}
[REL] the accrued amount on the card == the accrued amount in the modal for that same mission {SPEC} HELD, DELIBERATELY NOT UPGRADED: Phase 5 could observe this only as 0.00 == 0.00, an equality that a working mirror and a broken one both satisfy, so the observation does not decide the assertion. It also ran as USER_DEFAULT rather than as this case's own @td(MSN_E2E_USER_003.email) identity, so the claim was never exercised on its own fixture at all. What would decide it: a NON-ZERO accrual on that identity — at least one settled qualifying order against @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.name) — read on the card and in the modal within one pass. CHECKED 2026-09-08 AND STILL NOT AVAILABLE, so this stays HELD: the case's own fixture generation carries ZERO accrual by construction - @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.progress_percent_at_seed) is 0 with status @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.progress_status_at_seed), and @td(MSN_E2E_USER_003.balance_at_seed) is 0, because the e2e run mints a FRESH account per generation and the order-placing leg is 083d's, not this case's. The identity is deliberately NOT folded onto the mission-progress owner ({{USER_EMAIL}}), which does carry non-zero accrual: that would silently move this case off the fixture generation its other four assertions are grounded on. The route to grounding assertion 4 is a run of this case IMMEDIATELY AFTER 083d has placed a qualifying order on @td(MSN_E2E_USER_003.email) within the same seed generation - not a change of identity here
[FORMAT] each money figure on the card progress line renders with a currency symbol, with exactly two decimal places {OBSERVED}

[MATH] with only the row for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.sku) at a non-zero quantity, the cart subtotal = that row unit price × its stepper quantity, to two decimal places {BL}
[MATH] with both rows at a non-zero quantity, the cart subtotal = the sum over rows of unit price × stepper quantity, to two decimal places {BL}
[REL] in each of those two readings, the currency code on the subtotal == the currency code every contributing row carries, so the label does not follow whichever row happens to be collected first {BL}
[REL] POSITIVE CONTROL — in the modal for @td(MSN_E2E_PERSKU_PTS.name), whose single row is priced in a different currency from the rows of @td(MSN_E2E_PERSKU.name), the subtotal currency code == that row own currency code; two modals reporting the same subtotal currency code over rows that carry different ones show the label is fixed rather than row-derived {OBSERVED}
[REL] the total-units figure == the sum of the stepper values across the rows {SPEC}
[FORMAT] exactly one currency code appears across the rows and the subtotal of a single modal, so no modal renders a sum of amounts denominated differently {BL}
[REL] DECIDABILITY GATE — if both modals resolve to the same currency code because the environment prices every fixture alike, the control arm is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET and never PASS: a single-currency row set cannot distinguish a row-derived label from a constant {DOC}

[REL] at modal open, before any stepper is touched, the targets-met figure in the modal == the corresponding figure in the progress note on the card that opened it {SPEC}
[MATH] the targets-met denominator = the number of featured product rows the modal renders {SPEC}
[REL] the targets-met figure at open == the progress the loyaltyMissionProgress response returns for that mission on this load, so the counter reports the state the API returned {DOC}
[REL] the cart line count after both readings == the cart line count before them, so neither figure was moved by a cart change taken between the two observations {OBSERVED}
[DOM] the counter label in the modal and the progress note on the card are visible with wording consistent with the same underlying measurement, so the reader is never shown two counters that appear to disagree {SPEC}

[DOM] every points-history row visible on the page carries a reference naming the mission that granted it, so a reader can tell which mission paid each grant {BL}
[REL] the number of rows the page renders == the number of operation-log entries the response carries, so no row was dropped before the comparison {DOC}
[REL] POSITIVE CONTROL — at least one rendered row carries its order reference, which proves the row template renders references at all; where no row carries any reference the surface itself is not rendering and the case is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET rather than FAIL {OBSERVED}
[REL] for every mission whose card reads as completed, a points-history row exists naming that mission, matched on the mission name carrying the run handle @td(MSN_E2E_RUN.run_id) {BL}
[REL] attribution is per grant, never per balance: a balance delta alone is not accepted as evidence that a given mission paid, because one order settles several missions at once and their grants are indistinguishable in a total {BL}

[STATE] DECIDABILITY GATE, evaluated from the fixture and the selector before any modal price is read: @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.list_price) and @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.list_price_secondary) are both non-zero and differ from each other, @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency) differs from @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary), and the currency selector offers @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary). If any part of this is not satisfied the discriminating arm is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET and never PASS; a modal reading that merely fails to differ is a FAIL of this case, not a BLOCKED {OBSERVED}
[REL] POSITIVE CONTROL that the switch itself took effect — the independent control product @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_UNIT.sku), which no mission modal renders, reports a currency code on its product detail page after the switch that differs from the one it reported before it and equals @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary). A control that does not move proves the session currency never changed, so every arm downstream is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET rather than PASS {OBSERVED}
[MATH] DISCRIMINATOR, amount — modal row amount for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.sku) under session currency @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary) = @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.list_price_secondary). Neither hypothesised broken reading can satisfy this: AllPrices.FirstOrDefault() serves the same row it served in the default session, while a 1:1 exchange-rate conversion carries the default-currency amount across, so both render @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.list_price) here {BL}
[MATH] BASELINE, amount — modal row amount for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.sku) under the store default session currency = @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.list_price), carrying the currency code @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency), so the discriminator above is read against a baseline that is known good rather than against an assumption {BL}
[REL] DISCRIMINATOR, denomination — under session currency @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary) the modal row for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.sku) carries the currency code @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary). Paired with the amount assertion this separates the two broken implementations from each other: amount unchanged AND code unchanged is AllPrices.FirstOrDefault(); amount unchanged BUT code changed is a 1:1 exchange-rate conversion, which BL-PRICE-005 forbids independently of the missions surface {BL}
[REL] IN-MODAL CONTROL — under session currency @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary) the modal row for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.sku), which is priced in @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.currency) only, does NOT carry the currency code @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.currency). A row still denominated in the default currency proves the response was assembled from the default-currency price rows, whatever the dual-priced row happens to show, and it proves it inside the same modal response rather than across a second request {BL}
[STATE] Storefront honouring of the platform availability flags, outcome UNDECIDED on this build — under session currency @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary), where availabilityData.isAvailable and isBuyable are false for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.sku), the modal row presents as unavailable: no active quantity stepper and no active add-to-cart. A zero amount rendered ALONGSIDE a correct unavailable presentation is NOT a failure of this line — that zero is the platform no-secondary-pricelist condition, not a modal defect. BL-PRICE-005 is the origin of the obligation; what is tested here is the storefront honouring the flags, which the query already selects via the shared availabilityData fragment. Honoured => no finding; ignored => an in-scope mission-modal defect. Scored on its own line; it does not by itself decide the currency-selection arms above {BL}
[REL] under session currency @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.currency_secondary) the modal row price for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.sku) equals, in amount and currency code, the price the same SKU product detail page renders in that same session. Parity is a guard, not the discriminator — a wrong price is wrong on both surfaces — so it catches the modal being wrong DIFFERENTLY from the catalogue, never the modal being wrong the same way {BL}
[REL] under the store default session currency the modal row prices for @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_A.sku) and @td(MSN_E2E_PRODUCT_B.sku) each equal, in amount and currency code, the price the same SKU product detail page renders in that same session {BL}
[COUNT] in each of the two readings, exactly one distinct currency code appears across all product rows of the modal, so a mixed-currency row set is absent from a surface whose subtotal labels itself from a single row {BL}
[FORMAT] every modal row price renders with a currency symbol and exactly two digits after the decimal separator {BL}

[REL] ARM 1 — for the one mission @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.name), the currency code rendered against the progress-so-far amount == the currency code rendered against its goal target, so the two numbers a customer is asked to compare are denominated alike {BL}
[MATH] the goal currency code rendered against the target = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.goal_currency), the mission own stored goal currency {BL}
[MATH] the goal target amount rendered = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.goal_target), so the target figure is the mission stored value rather than a session-converted one {OBSERVED}
[REL] ARM 1 — after the session currency is switched, the two money fields for that one mission still agree with each other in currency code {BL}
[REL] ARM 1 DISCRIMINATOR — the goal target reading after the currency switch == the goal target reading before it, in both amount and currency code, because a goal is denominated by the mission and never by the session. A build that re-denominates the stored goal to the session currency fails here on the currency code, and a build that converts it at the registered exchange rate fails here on the amount {BL}
[STATE] ARM 2 DECIDABILITY GATE, evaluated from the selector and a NON-MONEY observation before any ARM 2 money field is compared: the language selector offered a locale other than the one active at sign-in, that locale was selected, and the document root lang attribute after the switch differs from the one recorded at sign-in. If either is not satisfied ARM 2 is BLOCKED:PRECONDITION_UNMET and never PASS, and ARM 1 still reports its own verdict {OBSERVED}
[REL] ARM 2 CONTROL — the currency code shown in the header currency selector after the locale switch == the code recorded at sign-in, so nothing ARM 2 observes is attributable to a session-currency change {OBSERVED}
[REL] ARM 2 DISCRIMINATOR — under the switched locale at unchanged session currency, the currency code rendered against a TOP-LEVEL mission money field on the card or order-goal modal for @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.name) == the currency code rendered against a PRODUCT money field, taken from the featured-SKU modal row where that modal rendered rows and otherwise from the corroborating product detail page reading. Top-level mission money is formatted from CultureName by AfterMediatorSend while product money resolves off currencyCode, so this is the only pair on this feature whose two members travel different inputs. A build that lets the locale drive denomination renders the two under different currency codes and fails here, while the ARM 1 pair — two top-level fields that both travel AfterMediatorSend — would still agree {BL}
[REL] ARM 2 — the currency code rendered against the top-level mission money under the switched locale == the currency code the same field rendered under the original locale, because a culture governs how a number is written and never which currency it is in {BL}
[MATH] ARM 2 — under the switched locale the goal target amount rendered = @td(MSN_E2E_ORDERVALUE.goal_target), so a locale change alters the presentation of the number and never the number {BL}
[FORMAT] in every reading both money fields render with a currency symbol and exactly two digits after the decimal separator of the locale then active, so a locale that uses a different decimal separator changes the separator and never the count of fractional digits {BL}
[REL] the case records which surface supplied each of the two readings in each arm; where only one of a compared pair is rendered anywhere, that comparison is NOT-DECIDABLE and is reported as such rather than as a PASS {SPEC}

[DOM] a success notification is present on that FIRST observation, so the disappearance below is an auto-dismiss rather than a notification that never rendered — this is the positive control without which the rest of the case passes vacuously {OBSERVED}
[NEG] the success notification is no longer present within the dismissal budget, with no pointer, keyboard or navigation interaction of any kind in between {OBSERVED}
[REL] the recorded elapsed time to disappearance is greater than zero and below the dismissal budget — the notification neither outlives the budget (which is the VCST-5909 defect: useNotifications.open() arms its timer only when a duration is passed, and it has no default) nor vanishes before the first observation, which would leave the confirmation unreadable {OBSERVED}
[STATE] the modal CLOSES as part of the add on the success path — pre-existing designed behaviour, not something PR #2471 introduced (close() sits unchanged as context immediately after the success notification in addProductsToCart(); the catch block carries none) — so the notification outlives the modal, and what must survive the auto-dismiss is the WRITE: the cart still holds the line the add created, confirmed on the cart page {OBSERVED}
[NEG] no second notification appeared at any point during the idle wait {OBSERVED}

[DOM] a success notification with a close control is present on the first observation {OBSERVED}
[NEG] the notification is no longer present on the observation immediately following the close click, so the close control is still wired {OBSERVED}
[REL] the elapsed time between the two recorded clocks is far below the auto-dismiss interval, so the disappearance is attributable to the close click and not to the timer — a case that cannot separate the two proves nothing about the close control {OBSERVED}
[STATE] closing the notification mutates nothing else — and note what the state already is by this point: the modal CLOSED as part of the add itself (pre-existing designed behaviour, close() unchanged as context on the success path in addProductsToCart()), so there is no open modal and no stepper left for this assertion to preserve. What must survive the close click is the WRITE: the cart still holds the line the add created {OBSERVED}

[MATH] at 375 pixels the name/info block inner width / the inner width of its product row = 0.5 or more — pre-fix that ratio was the residual sliver left over after the stepper, under a fifth of the row, which is the geometry the defect described {OBSERVED}
[STATE] at 375 pixels the stepper group is on its OWN row: its box starts below the bottom edge of the info block in the same product row, and it spans the row rather than sharing a line with the name {OBSERVED}
[REL] each product row identifies its own product: the subtitle under the row for @td(MSN_PERSKU_PRODUCT_A.sku) contains that SKU, the subtitle under the row for @td(MSN_PERSKU_PRODUCT_B.sku) contains that SKU, and the two subtitles differ from one another — so a customer can tell which stepper controls which product {OBSERVED}
